WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.200
Our entire medical system
really relies on antibiotics.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.240
This would be the biggest
health crisis

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.160
that we've experienced this century.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.120
We don't hear about it.
But it's already here.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.600
Nobody is safe, until we're all safe.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.120
What would a world without
antibiotics look like?

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.480
Well, I think it would look
a lot like the past,

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.840
where a lot of people
would die younger than they do now.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.840
Just think about war -

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.440
more people died of infections
and their wounds,

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.000
than died actually
on the battlefield.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.080
The discovery of various classes
of antibiotics in the 20th Century

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:00.120
had a profound impact on health care.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.880
So treatment of infection suddenly
became very straightforward,

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.560
and it's something
that we benefit a lot from today.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.320
Antibiotics protect people
during operative surgery -

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.480
Caesarean sections, replacement
joints, let alone cancer treatments. 

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.080
Antibiotics added, on average,
20 years life to everyone.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.160
We went to the hospital,
the emergency room.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.480
They said they gave him
a broad spectrum antibiotic.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.720
And then they took me
to another room and they're like,

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.920
"Your son has an infection.
We don't know the source." 

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.280
We were in the ICU
with like 10 doctors,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.360
and they said he wasn't
really going to make it. 

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.320
At that point, I knew
that he was dead. 

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.360
I could feel it.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.560
And that's when we learned
that Simon had contracted

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.240
an antibiotic resistant bacterium,
a superbug. 

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.920
And I had never heard
of any of this. 

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.760
Alexander Fleming's discovery
of penicillin was immense. 

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.400
Noticing that in a petri dish
where bacteria were growing,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.320
there were white areas where
the bacteria were not growing.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.800
And he realised that
something had happened.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.880
He looked and found
the fungus, penicillin -

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.000
our first effective antibiotic
that saved masses of lives. 

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.280
Most antibiotics come
from soil and fungi.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.200
When I expose bacteria
to antibiotics,

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:10.680
they're going to become resistant,

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.720
which is very bad because
we haven't discovered

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.680
a new antibiotic
in the last 30 years. 

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.960
Growing up back in Mexico,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.320
you didn't need a prescription
to get an antibiotic.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.760
You had a little bit
of a sore throat,

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.200
you go to the pharmacy
and get an antibiotic. 

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.440
And that only gives more and more
chances to these bugs

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.960
to acquire mutations
to become resistant.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:00.040
The situation today is more
serious than people realise.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.600
It's actually the first real study
looking at all the data -

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.120
494 million patient records -

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.400
to model what is happening.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.080
It is predicted that by 2050,

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:27.240
10 million people
are going to die every year

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.440
from complications with superbugs
or resistant microbes. 

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.240
So we really have
to find alternative strategies

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:35.880
to fight against these bugs.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.080
A world without antibiotics -

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.720
I sometimes call it
"the post-antibiotic apocalypse" -

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.120
would impact on our food chain too,

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:50.000
because animals would get ill,
plants would get ill and die.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.760
We would really
be in the most dreadful mess.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.040
As an individual, I think
one of the most important things

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.600
is don't ask for antibiotics
if they are not offered to you.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:06.360
If you are prescribed antibiotics,

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.040
then make sure you finish the course
of antibiotics that you're given.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.360
Because if you don't
finish the course,

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:13.840
even if you're feeling better,

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.560
there might be some
residual infection

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.200
that could become resistant.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.400
We have probably found
the easy-to-find antibiotics.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.000
But that doesn't mean there
are not many more to be found. 

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.080
If we keep recycling
the same old treatments,

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.800
then the problem is just
going to exacerbate. 

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.400
One of the main bottlenecks
with antibiotic research

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.840
is that the easiest thing to do

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:42.480
is to look at the structures
of existing antibiotics,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.160
and modify those slightly
to try to overcome the resistance.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:50.360
It's much more challenging to find a
completely new class of antibiotics.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:54.760
So we have to fund quite widely
in order to be able to identify

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.080
those strategies
that are going to work best. 

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.240
I think if there were more awareness

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.800
then there would be more
general pressure from society

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.800
on governments and on companies

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.200
to fund more research
into targeting this problem. 

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.160
We should be anticipating problems,

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.680
and doing something about them
before they become

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.280
enormous global crises.

